  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 AM EDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nA diffuse area of vorticity runs into the vigorous shortwave \nassociated with the nor'easter along the east coast and this allows \nfor minimal impacts on our dry weather at hand as surface high \npressure and mid level ridging continue to remain in control of the \nweather. 850 mb temperatures remain between 10 and 13C, warmest west \nof IN-15, until Tuesday night and really the key to temperatures \nincreasing will be low level lapse rates which are the best on \nMonday east of I-69, but also reach 7+C/km today and Tuesday. This \nhelps to keep high temperatures in the 70 to 75F range through \nMonday. Finally, warmer mid teens 850 mb temps work into the area \nTuesday through Thursday with mid 70s more common across our \nsouthwest counties and southern 1 row of counties. This is as a \nhigher theta-e plume swings through the area during this time frame.\n\nThe aforementioned theta-e plume also brings in enough moisture to \nreturn talk about rain in the forecast again. By the time we get to \nWednesday, the strong nor'easter along the east coast has pushed \neastward into the Atlantic and a diffuse area of vorticity \nassociated with a positively tilted trough aloft and a surface warm \nwarm front enters the area. These warm fronts are synonymous with \nhaving issues with dry air when it comes to causing precipitation, \nespecially when dry surface anticyclones have been in place overhead \nlike we've had. Models continue to struggle with timing the \nprecipitation into the area with the ECMWF being the only med range \nmodel causing precip across the area late Tue night into early Thu, \nand it appears to be quite dry in its own right. Finally, both the \nGFS/AIGFS/CMC all begin to push more rain into the area Thu and this \nis when the ECMWF finally brings its better moisture (65F dew \npoints, 10+C 850 mb dew points) through the area. During this whole \ntime, Tue through late work week, MUCAPE stays below 100 J/kg which \nmeans the convective element remains missing through this whole time \nperiod where rain chances are heightened. The ECMWF has a period of \nweak large scale ascent moving into the area Wednesday morning as \nwell as at times between Thursday and the weekend. Models now appear \nto be much slower bringing the cutoff low in the south northward \ntoward the area, waiting until the weekend to do so and that's why \nthe ECMWF has so much more large scale ascent to work with during \nthe weekend. As we head into the late week period, we'll also have \nto see where a cold front sets up because if it is just to our \nsouth, we likely avoid the better moisture advection into the area \nand surface high pressure is our main weather maker. In summary, for \nthis Wednesday into the weekend period, rain appears possible at \ntimes, but heavy rain and stronger storms appears harder to come by. \nThe greatest chance for heavy rain would be the later Friday through \nSunday period as long as a stalled front sets up just right across \nthe area. In line with the late week/weekend chance for rain, our \nhighs in the 70s forecast leading up to this period are expected to \nbe cooler, more like in the mid to upper 60s. At least our lows in \nthe 40s warm into the 50s and, at times, 60s with the rain around. \n\nThese rain chances look to be out of the area by the time we get to \nearly next week.",
